window.__registerChapter({"num": 28, "slug": "028_Pediatric_Cardiomyopathies", "title": "Pediatric Cardiomyopathies", "authors": "Stephanie M. Ware, Gabrielle C. Geddes", "part_num": "IV", "part_title": "Cardiopulmonary Disorders", "word_count": 9753, "pearls": ["The most common includes three categories: dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM), hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) and restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM).", "DCM, characterized by systolic dysfunction, dilation of the left ventricle, and normal left ventricular wall thickness, is the most common, occurring about twofold more frequently than HCM.", "In children the most common age of presentation of DCM is in infancy, followed by adolescence.", "HCM and RCM are both characterized by diastolic dysfunction and the failure of appropriate left ventricular relaxation.", "Due to the intrinsic failure of relaxation, RCM is characterized by elevated filling pressures and atrial enlargement (Fig. 28.2).", "Monogenic causes of isolated, or primary cardiomyopathy has an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern with high penetrance.", "Echocardiographic images are shown for all, except ARVC which has cardiac MRI diagnostic criteria.", "Inheritance is most commonly autosomal dominant and most of the known pathogenic variants responsible for DCM are missense and nonsense substitutions in protein coding sequence."], "genes": ["AARS2", "ACAD9", "ALMS1", "BCS1L", "COA5", "COQ2", "COQ4", "COQ9", "COX14", "COX6B1", "DMD", "DNAJC19", "DSC2", "DSP", "ELAC2", "FBXL4", "FHL1", "FOXRED1"], "omims": [], "subtopics": ["Isolated Cardiomyopathies", "Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy", "Dilated Cardiomyopathy", "Restrictive Cardiomyopathy", "Noncompaction Cardiomyopathy", "LEFT VENTRICULAR NONCOMPACTION (LVNC)", "Arrhythmogenic Cardiomyopathy", "Syndromic Cardiomyopathies", "RASopathies", "Causes of LVNC", "Alström Syndrome", "Revised Task Force Criteria for the Diagnosis of Arrhythmogenic Right Ventricular Cardiomyopathy", "Copy Number Variants", "Neuromuscular Diseases"], "figs": [{"img": "fig_p0634_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 28.1", "caption": "Relationships of genes implicated in causing cardiovascular and related phenotypes. The gene relationships for several cardiovascular phenotypes are shown, with the principal focus on dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM) genetics. Common cardiac phenotypes are shown in the purple ovals , and lines connect each phenotype to the gene or genes (shown in a box) of which rare variants have been implicated in causing the phenotype. The gene boxes are color-coded according to the number of phenotypes with which they are associated: blue indicates one phenotype, red indicates two phenotypes, and yellow indicates three phenotypes (as shown in the lower left corner of the figure). For a gene causing 3% or more of familial DCM cases, the frequency is included with its name. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) gene associations are indicated by dotted lines . Well-established HCM genes include two sarcomere genes ( MYH7 and MYBPC3 ) that together account for 80% of HCM cases for which a genetic cause can be identified. Three other sarcomere genes ( TNNT2 , TNNI3 , and TPM1 ) account for an additional 15% of such cases. The other numerous genes implicated have caused only one or a few reported cases. The evidence in support of rare variants in the genes shown and their relevance for the specified cardiomyopathy varies considerably. (From Hershberger RE: The dilated, restrictive, and infiltrative cardiomyopathies in Libby P, Bonow RO, Mann DL, et al., eds. _Bra"}, {"img": "fig_p0635_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 28.2", "caption": "Cardiomyopathy phenotypes and etiologies. Pediatric cardiomyopathy has five phenotypic classes including hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM), dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM), restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM), noncompaction cardiomyopathy (NCM), and arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy (ARVC) or ACM. Each phenotype has characteristic imaging findings. Echocardiographic images are shown for all, except ARVC which has cardiac MRI diagnostic criteria. In blue boxes below each phenotype, underlying etiologic categories are listed. Because of the rarity of RCM, NCM, and ARVC cases in the pediatric population, more often than not, a molecular genetic diagnosis or categorical diagnosis such as familial, syndromic, or metabolic is not made."}, {"img": "fig_p0636_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 28.3", "caption": "The MOGES nosology system for classifying cardiomyopathies. (M) Morphofunctional phenotype. This description is designed to contain additional information. These red flags are placed in parentheses after the notation of morphofunctional phenotype. (O) Organ/system involvement. This described involvement of other organs. (E) Etiologic annotation provides information on specific disease genes and variant, as well as a description of nongenetic causes of disease. (G) May denote a genetic disease, supporting family monitoring strategies. (S) Stage. This provides a simple description of functional limitation using existing scores such as the New York Heart Association class. ACC, American College of Cardiology; AHA, American Heart Association; ARVC/D, arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy/dysplasia; DCM, dilated cardiomyopathy; ECG, electrocardiogram; ECHO, echocardiogram; HCM, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; LVNC, left ventricular noncompaction; NYHA, New York Heart Association; RCM, restrictive cardiomyopathy. (Used with permission of Elsevier from: J Am Coll Cardiol . 2014;64:304318. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2014.05.027, permission conveyed through Copyright Clearance Center, Inc. From Elliott P. Towards a new classification of cardiomyopathies. Curr Cardiol Rep 2023;25:229-233 [Fig. 1, p. 231].)"}, {"img": "vfig_p0635_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 28.5", "caption": "Graphic distribution of subtypes of pediatric cardiomyopathy aligned with most relevant"}, {"img": "fig_p0637_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 28.5", "caption": "Graphic distribution of subtypes of pediatric cardiomyopathy aligned with most relevant"}, {"img": "vfig_p0636_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 28.5", "caption": "Graphic distribution of subtypes of pediatric cardiomyopathy aligned with most relevant"}, {"img": "vfig_p0636_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 28.5", "caption": "Graphic distribution of subtypes of pediatric cardiomyopathy aligned with most relevant"}, {"img": "fig_p0638_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 28.5", "caption": "Graphic distribution of subtypes of pediatric cardiomyopathy aligned with most relevant"}, {"img": "fig_p0644_03.jpg", "label": "Fig. 28.6", "caption": "Muscular dystrophies with cardiac involvement. Genes that are expressed in both cardiac and skeletal muscle can lead to muscular dystrophy and cardiomyopathy when abnormal. Three major categories of muscular dystrophies associated with cardiomyopathy are Duchenne/Becker, Emery-Dreifuss, and limb-girdle muscular dystrophies. Not shown are congenital muscular dystrophies which may also be associated with cardiomyopathy. These muscular dystrophies have different patterns of weakness based on the specific skeletal muscles affected in each disorder. Muscles primarily affected in each of the muscular dystrophies are shaded in the diagrams."}, {"img": "fig_p0648_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 28.7", "caption": "Examples of mitochondrial defects in patients with cardiomyopathy. (A) Scanning electron micrograph (SEM) from a cardiac biopsy obtained from a 7-year-old male with apparently isolated HCM subsequently shown to have a significantly elevated lactate and abnormal plasma amino acids. SEM shows sheets of intermyofibrillar mitochondria and a paucity of cardiac myofibrils. Mitochondrial genome sequencing revealed m.3303C>T tRNA pathogenic variant that was 97% heteroplasmic. (B and C) Skeletal muscle biopsy from a 2-year-old patient with mitochondrial DNA depletion. Presentation was at 6 months of age with cardiogenic shock and arrhythmia, metabolic acidosis, and severe growth restriction. Histology (B) shows abnormalities including central nuclei (arrows) and degeneration (asterisk) . SEM (C) shows pathological mitochondrial throughout including increased neutral lipid and glycogen (not shown in panel), Z-band streaming, increased mitochondrial size, and bizarre shaped mitochondria (arrows) ."}, {"img": "fig_p0650_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 28.8", "caption": "Clinical approach to cardiomyopathies. Cardiomyopathy phenotypes can be roughly categorized according to ventricular morphology and function. After ruling out systemic and environmental/lifestyle causes of cardiomyopathy, clinicians should consider genetic etiologies. A family history of heart failure, cardiomyopathy, or sudden death in a first-degree relative suggests a possible monogenic familial disease. Unique additional cardiac (i.e., arrhythmia, conduction defect, or congenital lesion) and extracardiac (i.e., skeletal myopathy) features may guide the genetic evaluation; a genetic testing strategy may start with a focus on the gene(s) specifically linked to a single highly suspected endotype (i.e., TTR gene when hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis is high on the differential, perhaps due to the presence of an abnormal nuclear scintigraphy scan), or may involve use of a broader multi gene panel. Clinicians should ensure that the selected test includes the gene(s) and methodologies (i.e., need for repeat expansion testing for DMPK in patients with suspected myotonic dystrophy type 1) necessary for diagnoses. The dagger indicates the requirement of repeat expansion analysis. AL, Amyloid light chain; ASD, atrial septal defect; CCD, cardiac conduction disease; CPK, creatine phosphokinase; GI, gastrointestinal; LVH, left ventricular hypertrophy; PS, pulmonic stenosis; SCD, sudden cardiac death; VA, ventricular arrhythmia; WPW, Wolf"}], "tables_meta": [{"label": "Table 28.1", "title": "Type Morphology Phenome Physiology Pathology Systemic Conditions, Clinical Features, Risk Factors Genome Nonsyndromic, U"}, {"label": "Table 28.2", "title": "Phenome Genome"}, {"label": "TABLE 28.3", "title": "Causes of DCM"}, {"label": "TABLE 28.4", "title": "#### 28.4 Causes of Inflammatory Dilated Cardiomyopathy"}, {"label": "TABLE 28.9", "title": "Causes of Arrhythmogenic Cardiomyopathy"}, {"label": "TABLE 28.10", "title": "Gene Variants and Cardiac Manifes tations of Neurom uscular Disorders"}, {"label": "Table 28.7", "title": "Condition Genetic Defect Heart Findings Other Clinical Features"}, {"label": "Table 28.8", "title": "TABLE Mitochondrial DNA Abnormalities Associated With Cardiomyopathy and Arrhythmias or Conduction"}, {"label": "Table 28.9", "title": "Condition Genetic Defect Heart Findings Other Clinical Features"}, {"label": "TABLE 28.13", "title": "Specia i Includ l Cases for Genotype-Specific Considerations in ing Genes Associated With Unique Cardiac and N i Clinical Man oncardiac F i "}, {"label": "TABLE 28.11", "title": "Metabolic Testing for Pediatric Cardiomyopathies 28.14"}], "body_md": "\n<!-- p.630 -->\n\n# 28 Pediatric Cardiomyopathies \n\n**STEPHANIE M. WARE, GABRIELLE C. GEDDES** \n\nPediatric cardiomyopathies are diseases of the heart muscle that have etiologic and phenotypic heterogeneity. The pathologic injury of cardiomyocytes leading to cardiomyopathy can result from viral infection, toxins (e.g., chemotherapy, alcohol), hypertension, immune or inflammatory disorders, ischemia, arrhythmias, or genetic disorders. Among genetic causes of cardiomyopathy, cardiologists have often distinguished **primary cardiomyopathies** , monogenic disorders resulting from pathogenic variants in sarcomeric or cytoskeletal components of the cardiomyocyte, from **secondary cardiomyopathies** which result from systemic disorders and include genetic conditions with cardiomyopathy as part of multiple malformation syndromes, inborn errors of metabolism, and neuromuscular diseases (Table 28.1, Fig. 28.1). The overall incidence of pediatric cardiomyopathy in the United States is ~1.1 for 100,000 person years for those under the age of 20 years. This increases significantly for infants, with an estimated incidence of 8.3 for 100,000 person years for those under the age of one year. Cardiomyopathy is a condition with high morbidity and mortality; pediatric presentation of symptomatic cardiomyopathy is associated with 2-year transplant-free survival of just over 60%. \n\nThere are a number of proposed classification systems for pediatric cardiomyopathy based on phenotype and genotype (Table 28.1). The most common includes three categories: dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM), hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) and restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM). In pediatrics there typically is inclusion of two additional classifications, arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (ARVC), currently termed arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy (ACM), and left ventricular noncompaction cardiomyopathy (LVNC), currently termed noncompaction cardiomyopathy (NCM) (Fig. 28.2). Another classification system emphasizes morphology/function, organ involvement, genetics, etiology, and stage (Fig. 28.3). \n\nPediatric cardiomyopathies have significant genetic overlap with arrythmias and congenital heart disease (Fig. 28.1). Certain accompanying cardiac features, such as a shortened PR interval in Pompe disease or pulmonary valve stenosis in RASopathies, can help narrow the differential diagnosis during initial evaluation. \n\n## **Isolated Cardiomyopathies** \n\nIsolated, or primary cardiomyopathies in children (Fig. 28.4) have an age specific time of presentation. DCM, characterized by systolic dysfunction, dilation of the left ventricle, and normal left ventricular wall thickness, is the most common, occurring about twofold more frequently than HCM. In children the most common age of presentation of DCM is in infancy, followed by adolescence. HCM and RCM are both characterized by diastolic dysfunction and the failure of appropriate left ventricular relaxation. In HCM this is accompanied by a thickened left ventricular myocardium, most characteristically with asymmetric septal hypertrophy, whereas RCM, which comprises ~5% of pediatric cardiomyopathies, has normal thickness of the ventricles. Due to the intrinsic failure of relaxation, RCM is characterized by elevated filling pressures and atrial enlargement (Fig. 28.2). The incidence of HCM increases with age, but infants affected with HCM resulting from variants in sarcomeric genes are routinely identified at pediatric hospitals. In North America, there are ancestry-related phenotypic differences with DCM being more common in Black individuals and HCM more common in White individuals. ACM is very rare in children. \n\n## **Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy** \n\nMonogenic causes of isolated, or primary cardiomyopathy has an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern with high penetrance. _MYH7_ , the gene that encodes β myosin heavy chain, a component of the thick filament of the sarcomere and the structural protein containing the ATPase activity necessary for contraction, was the first gene identified as causative in families with HCM. Subsequently, pathogenic variants in _MYBPC3_ , _TNNT2_ , _ACTC_ , _MYL2_ , _TPM1_ , and other components of the thick and thin filaments of the sarcomere, their interacting proteins, or contiguous Z-disc were identified as causative in individuals with HCM. In adults, HCM occurs in approximately 1 in 500 individuals. It is firmly established that children share these genetic causes of HCM with adults and that significant locus and allelic heterogeneity exist (Figs. 28.1 and 28.5). Pathogenic variants are\n\n<!-- p.631 -->\n\n#### **TABLE 28.1 Classification of the Cardiomyopathies by Phenome and Genome** \n\n|Type|Morphology|Phenome<br>Physiology|Pathology|Systemic Conditions,<br>Clinical Features, Risk<br>Factors|Genome<br>Nonsyndromic,<br>Usually Single Gene|Syndromic|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Dilated (DCM)|Dilation of LV or LV<br>and RV with<br>minimal or no wall<br>thickening|Reduced<br>contractility is<br>the primary<br>defect; variable<br>degree of<br>diastolic<br>dysfunction|Myocyte<br>hypertrophy;<br>scattered<br>fibrosis|Hypertension; alcohol;<br>thyrotoxicosis,<br>myxedema; persistent<br>tachycardia; toxins (e.g.,<br>chemotherapy, especially<br>anthracyclines); radiation|Diverse gene ontology<br>with>30 genes<br>implicated|Diverse array of associated<br>conditions, especially<br>muscular dystrophies:<br>Emery-Dreifuss muscular<br>dystrophy, limb-girdle<br>muscular dystrophy,<br>Duchenne/Becker<br>muscular dystrophy;<br>Laing distal myopathy;<br>Barth syndrome; Kearns-<br>Sayre syndrome; others|\n|Restrictive (RCM)|Usually normal<br>chamber sizes;<br>minimal to<br>moderate wall<br>thickening|Contractility normal<br>or near-normal<br>with a marked<br>increase in end-<br>diastolic filling<br>pressure|Specific to type,<br>diagnosis:<br>amyloid, iron,<br>glycogen<br>storage<br>disease, others|Endomyocardial fibrosis,<br>amyloid, sarcoid,<br>scleroderma, EGPA<br>syndrome, cystinosis,<br>lymphoma,<br>pseudoxanthoma<br>elasticum,<br>hypereosinophilic<br>syndrome, carcinoid|If not associated with<br>systemic genetic<br>disease, genetic<br>cause usually from<br>sarcomeric gene rare<br>variants|Gaucher disease,<br>hemochromatosis, Fabry<br>disease, familial<br>amyloidosis;<br>mucopolysaccharidoses,<br>Noonan syndrome|\n|Hypertrophic<br>(HCM)|Usually normal or<br>reduced internal<br>chamber<br>dimension; wall<br>thickening<br>pronounced,<br>especially septal<br>hypertrophy|Systolic function<br>increased or<br>normal|Myocyte<br>hypertrophy,<br>classically with<br>disarray|Severe hypertension can<br>confound clinical,<br>morphologic diagnosis|Rare variants of genes<br>encoding sarcomeric<br>proteins|Noonan syndrome,<br>Danon syndrome,<br>Fabry disease,<br>Wolff-Parkinson-White<br>syndrome, Friedreich ataxia,<br>MERRF, MELAS|\n|Arrhythmogenic<br>right ventricular<br>cardiomyopathy<br>(ARVC)|Scattered fibrofatty<br>infiltration,<br>classically of RV<br>but also of LV;<br>dilation of RV or<br>LV, or both, is<br>common but not<br>universal|Ventricular<br>arrhythmias (VT,<br>VF) early or late,<br>reduced<br>contractility with<br>progressive<br>disease; can<br>mimic DCM|Islands of fatty<br>replacement;<br>fibrosis|Palmoplantar keratoderma,<br>wooly hair in Naxos<br>syndrome|Rare variants of genes<br>encoding proteins of<br>desmosome|Naxos syndrome|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.632 -->\n\n#### **TABLE 28.1 Classification of the Cardiomyopathies by Phenome and Genome—cont’d** \n\n|||Phenome|||Genome||\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Type|Morphology|Physiology|Pathology|Systemic Conditions,<br>Clinical Features, Risk<br>Factors|Nonsyndromic,<br>Usually Single Gene|Syndromic|\n|Inflammatory|Normal or dilated<br>without hypertrophy|Reduced systolic<br>function|Inflammatory<br>infiltrates|Hypereosinophilic syndrome,<br>acute myocarditis|||\n|Ischemic|Normal or dilated<br>without<br>hypertrophy|Reduced systolic<br>function|Areas of infarcted<br>myocardium|Hypercholesterolemia,<br>hypertension, diabetes,<br>cigarette smoking, family<br>history|Familial<br>hypercholesterolemia;<br>other heritable lipid<br>disorders|Familial<br>hypercholesterolemia|\n|Infectious|Normal or dilated<br>without<br>hypertrophy|Reduced systolic<br>function|Specific to<br>infection|Viral (especially acute<br>myocarditis); protozoal<br>(e.g., Chagas disease);<br>bacterial, direct infection<br>(e.g., Lyme disease) or<br>from acute cellular<br>toxicity as result of<br>systemic toxins (e.g.,<br>rheumatic fever, gram-<br>negative, others)|Genetic predisposition<br>to infection and/or<br>variable response to<br>infective agent||\n\n\n_EGPA,_ Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis; _LV,_ left ventricle; _MELAS,_ mitochondrial encephalopathy, lactic acidosis, and stroke like symptoms; _MERRF,_ myoclonic epilepsy associated with ragged-red fibers; _RV,_ right ventricle; _VF,_ ventricular fibrillation; _VT,_ ventricular tachycardia. \n\nFrom Hershberger RE. The dilated, restrictive, and infiltrative cardiomyopathies In: Libby P, Bonow RO, Mann DL, et al., eds. _Braunwald’s Heart Disease_ . 12th ed. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2022 (Table 52.1, p. 1033).\n\n<!-- p.633 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0634_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 28.1** Relationships of genes implicated in causing cardiovascular and related phenotypes. The gene relationships for several cardiovascular phenotypes are shown, with the principal focus on dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM) genetics. Common cardiac phenotypes are shown in the _purple ovals_ , and _lines_ connect each phenotype to the gene or genes (shown in a box) of which rare variants have been implicated in causing the phenotype. The gene boxes are color-coded according to the number of phenotypes with which they are associated: _blue_ indicates one phenotype, _red_ indicates two phenotypes, and _yellow_ indicates three phenotypes (as shown in the lower left corner of the figure). For a gene causing 3% or more of familial DCM cases, the frequency is included with its name. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) gene associations are indicated by _dotted lines_ . Well-established HCM genes include two sarcomere genes ( _MYH7_ and _MYBPC3_ ) that together account for 80% of HCM cases for which a genetic cause can be identified. Three other sarcomere genes ( _TNNT2_ , _TNNI3_ , and _TPM1_ ) account for an additional 15% of such cases. The other numerous genes implicated have caused only one or a few reported cases. The evidence in support of rare variants in the genes shown and their relevance for the specified cardiomyopathy varies considerably. (From Hershberger RE: The dilated, restrictive, and infiltrative cardiomyopathies in Libby P, Bonow RO, Mann DL, et al., eds. _Braunwald’s Heart Disease_ . 12th ed. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2022 [Table Fig. 52.2, p. 1034].) \n\ntypically private to a particular family. The reason for interand intrafamilial variability related to age of onset and severity of disease has not been elucidated but is presumed to be related to genetic modifiers especially in the pediatric population where environmental factors and comorbidities have less impact. In the largest study to date exclusively in the pediatric \n\npopulation with idiopathic or familial HCM, 51.3% of children under 18 years of age (average age of diagnosis is 11 years) had identifiable genetic causes, primarily in sarcomeric genes. Of those with a molecular diagnosis, approximately 70% have a causative variant in _MYH7_ or _MYBPC3_ . Other (secondary) etiologies of HCM are noted in Table 28.2.\n\n<!-- p.634 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0635_02.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 28.2** Cardiomyopathy phenotypes and etiologies. Pediatric cardiomyopathy has five phenotypic classes including hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM), dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM), restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM), noncompaction cardiomyopathy (NCM), and arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy (ARVC) or ACM. Each phenotype has characteristic imaging findings. Echocardiographic images are shown for all, except ARVC which has cardiac MRI diagnostic criteria. In blue boxes below each phenotype, underlying etiologic categories are listed. Because of the rarity of RCM, NCM, and ARVC cases in the pediatric population, more often than not, a molecular genetic diagnosis or categorical diagnosis such as familial, syndromic, or metabolic is not made. \n\n## **Dilated Cardiomyopathy** \n\nPrimary DCM has often been referred to as a disease of the cytoskeleton. However, genes responsible for isolated DCM control a variety of functions of the cardiomyocyte including structural components of the sarcomere ( _MYH7_ , _MYBPC3_ , _TPM1 TNNI3_ ; Figs. 28.1 and 28.5), cytoskeleton _(TTN)_ , nuclear envelope, chaperones, RNA splicing, and other mechanisms; there are no genes that account for more than 10% of cases and most account for less than 3%. In adults, pathogenic variants in _TTN_ encoding the giant protein TITIN cause 10 to 20% of cases and are also associated with peripartum cardiomyopathy. However, in children _TTN_ variants account for less than 5% of DCM cases. Inheritance is most commonly autosomal dominant and most of the known pathogenic variants responsible for DCM are missense and nonsense substitutions in protein coding sequence. Diagnostic (genetic) yield for DCM is approximately 20%. The lack of identification of pathogenic variants even in families with dominant inheritance patterns makes it clear that additional causes remain to be identified. In adults, some genotype-phenotype correlations have been useful for clinical management. Individuals with _LMNA_ pathogenic variants are more likely to require transplantation due to arrhythmogenic disease. \n\nFilamins are a group of large proteins important for crosslinking actin filaments and anchoring membrane proteins to the cytoskeleton. Pathogenic variants in _FLNC_ , the gene encoding Filamin C, have been reported in DCM, HCM and \n\nRCM as well as skeletal muscle myopathies (Fig. 28.1). _FLNC_ variants causing DCM have the most direct evidence of a genotype-phenotype relationship with truncating _FLNC_ variants (stop codons, frameshifts and splice site variants) most strongly associated with DCM, often with arrhythmia and increased risk for sudden death. Other (secondary) etiologies of DCM are noted in Tables 28.3 and 28.4). \n\n## **Restrictive Cardiomyopathy** \n\nRCM comprises ~5% of pediatric cardiomyopathy cases. Because of its rarity, genetic diagnostic yield is uncertain but pathogenic variants in _TNNI3_ followed by _MYH7_ genes are the most frequently identified causes. The Pediatric Cardiomyopathy Registry database identified 4.5% of its registry cases as RCM; 66% were pure RCM, and 34% there was a mixed phenotype. Age at diagnosis was not different between groups, but 10% of the pure RCM group was diagnosed in infancy versus 24% of the RCM/HCM group. One-, 2-, and 5-year survival of RCM is 82%, 80%, and 68%, respectively. Overall, in RCM ~50% of patients undergo transplants within 3 years of diagnosis. Other (secondary) causes of RCM are noted in Table 28.5. \n\n## **Noncompaction Cardiomyopathy** \n\nThe hallmark feature of NCM, also called LVNC, is hypertrabeculation of the left ventricle and failure of compaction\n\n<!-- p.635 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0636_01.jpg)\n\n\n> **Fig. 28.3** The MOGES nosology system for classifying cardiomyopathies. (M) Morphofunctional phenotype. This description is designed to contain additional information. These red flags are placed in parentheses after the notation of morphofunctional phenotype. (O) Organ/system involvement. This described involvement of other organs. (E) Etiologic annotation provides information on specific disease genes and variant, as well as a description of nongenetic causes of disease. (G) May denote a genetic disease, supporting family monitoring strategies. (S) Stage. This provides a simple description of functional limitation using existing scores such as the New York Heart Association class. _ACC,_ American College of Cardiology; _AHA,_ American Heart Association; _ARVC/D,_ arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy/dysplasia; _DCM,_ dilated cardiomyopathy; _ECG,_ electrocardiogram; _ECHO,_ echocardiogram; _HCM,_ hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; _LVNC,_ left ventricular noncompaction; _NYHA,_ New York Heart Association; _RCM,_ restrictive cardiomyopathy. (Used with permission of Elsevier from: _J Am Coll Cardiol_ . 2014;64:304318. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2014.05.027, permission conveyed through Copyright Clearance Center, Inc. From Elliott P. Towards a new classification of cardiomyopathies. _Curr Cardiol Rep_ 2023;25:229-233 [Fig. 1, p. 231].)\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0635_01.jpg)\n\n\n<!-- p.636 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0637_02.jpg)\n\n\n- **Fig. 28.5** Graphic distribution of subtypes of pediatric cardiomyopathy aligned with most relevant \n\n- genetic etiologies: (A) Dilated Cardiomyopathy. (B) Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy. \n\n_(Continued)_\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0636_01.jpg)\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0636_02.jpg)\n\n\n<!-- p.637 -->\n\n#### **LEFT VENTRICULAR NONCOMPACTION (LVNC)** \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0638_02.jpg)\n\n\n#### **TABLE Causes of HCM 28.2** \n\n_CoA,_ Coenzyme A; _GSD,_ glycogen storage disease; _HCM,_ hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; _PRKAG2,_ γ-2 subunit of AMP-activated protein kinase.-2 subunit of AMP-activated protein kinase. Reprinted with permission _Circulation_ . 2019;140:e9-e68 ©2019 American Heart Association, Inc.\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0637_01.jpg)\n\n\n<!-- p.638 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 28.3** Causes of DCM\n\n|**Primary DCM**|\n|---|\n|Familial/genetic|\n|Sarcomeric<br>|\n|Mitochondrial diseases|\n|Neuromuscular disorders|\n|Laminopathies|\n|**Secondary DCM**<br>l|\n|Inflammatory (seeTable 28.4)|\n|Toxins<br>|\n|Iron|\n|Lead|\n|Cobalt|\n|Arsenic|\n|Anthracycline|\n|Radiation|\n|Metabolic disorders|\n|Endocrinopathies|\n|Thyroid disorders|\n|Catecholamine-secreting tumor|\n|Parathyroid disease|\n|Diabetes mellitus|\n|Fatty acid oxidation disorders<br>i|\n|Carnitine deficiency<br>i|\n|Malonyl coenzyme decarboxylase deficiency|\n|GSDs|\n|GSD type II (Pompe disease)|\n|GSD type IV (Andersen disease)|\n|Lysosomal storage disorders|\n|Gaucher disease|\n|Mucopolysaccharidoses|\n|Sphingolipidoses|\n|Nutritional disorders<br>i|\n|Thiamine deficiency<br>i|\n|Selenium deficiency|\n|Protein energy malnutrition|\n|Structural heart diseases|\n|Valvular heart disease|\n|Ischemia: coronary artery anomalies, coronary|\n|artery injury|\n|Single ventricle|\n|Pulmonary diseases|\n|_DCM,_Dilated cardiomyopathy;_GSD,_glycogen storage disease|\n|Reprinted with permission_Circulation_. 2019;140:e9-e68 ©2019 American<br>Heart Association, Inc.|\n\n\n(Table 28.6). The right ventricle in a normal heart is typically relatively smooth. The normal trabeculation and compaction processes occur during embryonic development such that NCM can be considered a congenital disorder in some cases and may be associated with structural heart defects. Hypertrabeculation can also occur postnatally in certain disease states such as neuromuscular and mitochondrial disorders. Hypertrabeculation is also identified in conditions related to excessive preload such as occur with pregnancy or athletic training. Whether LVNC is a cardiomyopathy or a morphological trait or phenotype has been subject to debate. Noncompaction of the left ventricle can be seen in isolation or in conjunction with HCM, \n\nDCM, or RCM. When seen in conjunction with another cardiomyopathy type, medical management is based on those features. Adults with incidentally noted excessive trabeculation frequently require no further follow-up. In pediatric patients, it is prudent to obtain a careful family history for cardiomyopathy. In one pediatric study of successive patients with isolated or mixed NCM, no patients with isolated NCM without a family history of cardiomyopathy had positive genetic testing. Other etiologies of LVNC are noted in Table 28.7. \n\n## **Arrhythmogenic Cardiomyopathy** \n\nACM is characterized by fatty infiltration of the myocardium and fibrosis. Sudden cardiac death resulting from ventricular arrhythmias is a hallmark feature (Table 28.8). Affected patients may present with palpitations or syncope and there is a 3:1 male to female ratio. Abnormalities of the cardiac desmosome cause many cases of ACM with pathogenic variants in plakophilin 2 _(PKP2)_ being the most common. Presentation in childhood is exceedingly rare. Other (secondary) causes of ACM are noted in Table 28.9. \n\n## **Syndromic Cardiomyopathies** \n\n### **RASopathies** \n\nRASopathies refer to a group of _multiple malformation genetic_ conditions that have a common underlying molecular etiology of overactivation of the RAS-mitogen-activated-proteinkinase pathway. These genetic conditions have many overlapping features but are distinct disorders; they include diagnoses such as **cardiofaciocutaneous syndrome** , **Costello syndrome** , **Noonan syndrome** , **Noonan syndrome with loose anagen hair** , and **Noonan syndrome with multiple lentigines** . All of these diagnoses are associated with risk for HCM. There are specific high risk associated genes; _RAF1_ related Noonan syndrome and _RIT1_ related Noonan syndrome generally have the highest risk for HCM of the RASopathies. Cardiomyopathy in patients with RASopathies can be associated with high mortality. Infants with Noonan syndrome who presented with HCM and congestive heart failure before 6 months of age have a poor outcome. \n\nTreatment of HCM due to RASopathy has improved due to repurposing mTOR inhibitors and MEK inhibitors to directly address the underlying molecular mechanism causing RASopathies. The MEK inhibitor, trametinib, has shown that some patients with RASopathy can respond favorably to treatment with improvement in HCM. Trametinib has been successfully utilized in RASopathy patients to treat atrial tachyarrhythmias, chylous effusions, lymphatic anomalies, and seizures. Using the mTOR inhibitors sirolimus and everolimus for HCM in RASopathies is another option to improve outcomes. It is thought that mTOR inhibitors are helpful in patients with _PTPN11_ related Noonan syndrome with multiple lentigines because these inhibitors may inactivate the phosphatase activity.\n\n<!-- p.639 -->\n\n###### **TABLE** \n\n#### **28.4 Causes of Inflammatory Dilated Cardiomyopathy** \n\n|Causal Agent or Exposu|re|\n|---|---|\n|**Commonly Reported**<br>Infectious Causes<br>Bacterial||\n|Fungal||\n|Viral|Adenoviruses, enteroviruses, herpes simplex virus, varicella-zoster virus, human cytomegalovirus,<br>Epstein-Barr virus, human herpesvirus 6, influenza A and B viruses, HIV, parvovirus B19|\n|Rickettsial||\n|Spirochete|_Borrelia burgdorferi_|\n|Noninfectious Causes||\n|Autoimmune diseases|Dermatomyositis, hypereosinophilic syndrome, Kawasaki disease, lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis|\n|Hypersensitive reactions||\n|Drug reactions||\n|Toxic reactions to drugs|Penicillin, ampicillin, cephalosporins, tetracyclines, sulfonamides, anthracyclines|\n|Other exposures||\n|**Rarely Reported**||\n|Infectious Causes||\n|Bacterial|Chlamydia,_Corynebacterium diphtheria_, legionella,_Mycobacterium tuberculosis_, mycoplasma,<br>staphylococcus, group A streptococcus, pneumococcus|\n|Other|Actinomyces, aspergillus, candida,_Cryptococcus_, Helminthic:_Echinococcus granulosus, Trichinella_<br>_spiralis_, Protozoal:_Toxoplasma gondii, Trypanosoma cruzi_|\n|Viral|Variola, vaccinia, mumps, measles, rubella, hepatitis C, coronavirus, respiratory syncytial|\n|Rickettsial|_Coxiella brunetti, Rickettsia typhi_|\n|Spirochete|Leptospira,_Treponema pallidum_|\n|Noninfectious Causes<br>Autoimmune diseases|Celiac disease, EGPA, Crohn disease, giant-cell myocarditis, lymphofollicular myocarditis, sarcoidosis,<br>scleroderma, ulcerative colitis|\n|Toxic, direct, or<br>hypersensitivity<br>reactions|Antiphlogistics*, benzodiazepines, clozapine, loop and thiazide diuretics, methyldopa, smallpox<br>vaccine, tetanus toxoid, tricyclic antidepressants, amphetamines, catecholamines, cocaine,<br>cyclophosphamide, 5-fluorouracil, phenytoin, trastuzumab, ethanol|\n|Other exposures|Arsenic, copper, iron, radiotherapy, thyrotoxicosis|\n\n\n*Antiinflammatory agents. _EGPA,_ Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis. Kühl U, Schultheiss HP. Myocarditis in children. _Heart Fail Clin_ . 2010;6:483-496, viii. doi: 10.1016/j.hfc.2010.05.009. \n\n**TABLE Causes of RCM 28.5 Primary RCM** Lysosomal storage disorder Genetic Anderson-Fabry disease Sarcomeric Iron overload Desmin Endomyocardial fibrosis Filamin-C Parasitic infection Autoimmune disorders **Secondary RCM** Malignancy with hypereosinophilia Infiltrative Possible dietary deficiencies or ingested toxins in certain Amyloidosis areas of endemic disease \n\n_RCM,_ Restrictive cardiomyopathy \n\nReprinted with permission _Circulation_ . 2019;140:e9-e68 ©2019 American Heart Association, Inc.\n\n<!-- p.640 -->\n\n#### **TABLE Echocardiographic and Magnetic Resonance Imaging Diagnostic Criteria for Left Ventricular 28.6 Noncompaction** \n\n##### **Echocardiographic Criteria** \n\n##### Chin et al. (California Criteria)* \n\nLVNC is defined by a ratio of the distance from the epicardial surface to trabecular recess : epicardial surface to tip of the trabeculation less than 0.5 \n\nThese criteria evaluate trabeculae at the LV apex on the parasternal short axis and apical views and by using the LV free wall thickness at end-diastole \n\n##### Jenni et al. (Zurich Criteria)<sup>†</sup> \n\nBilayered myocardium consisting of a thin C layer and a much thicker NC layer with deep endomyocardial recesses: NC/C >2 Predominant location of the pathology is midlateral, midinferior, and at the apex Evidence of intertrabecular recesses filled with blood from the LV cavity Acquisition of image views short axis with measurement of the NC/C ratio performed at end-systole \n\n##### Stöllberger and Finsterer (Vienna Criteria)<sup>‡</sup> \n\nFour or more trabeculations protruding from the LV wall, located apical to the papillary muscles and visible in one imaging plane Trabeculations with the same echogenicity as the myocardium and synchronous movement with ventricular contractions Perfusion of the intertrabecular recesses from the LV cavity Acquisition of images in the apical four-chamber view, atypical views to obtain the best-quality image for differentiation between false chords, aberrant bands, and trabeculations \n\n##### Paterick et al. (Milwaukee Criteria)<sup>§</sup> \n\nEvaluation of trabeculation sizes (NC myocardium) in relation to C wall thicknesses in multiple imaging windows and at different ventricular levels throughout the cardiac cycle \n\nIdentification of the bilayered myocardium (C and NC) in short-axis views at the mid and apical levels and in the apical two-and four-chamber and apical long-axis views \n\nThicknesses of the C and NC sections of the myocardium are best measured in short-axis views at end-diastole, with an NC/C ratio >2 being diagnostic of LVNC \n\n##### **Magnetic Resonance Criteria** \n\nPetersen et al.<sup>B</sup> \n\nRatio between NC and Clayers >2.3 at end-diastole \n\n##### Jacquier et al.<sup>¶</sup> \n\nTrabeculated LV mass >20% of global LV mass (measurements made at end-diastole \n\n> *Chin TK, Perloff JK, Williams RG, et al. Isolated noncompaction of left ventricular myocardium: a study of eight cases. _Circulation_ , 1990, 82:507 †Jenni ER, Oechslin E, Schneider J, et al. Echocardiographic and pathoanatomical characteristics of isolated left ventricular non-compaction: a step towards classification as a distinct cardiomyopathy. _Heart_ , 2001;86:666. \n\n> ‡Stollberger C, Finsterer J. Left ventricular hypertrabeculation/noncompaction. _J Am Soc Echocardiogr_ . 2004;17:91. \n\n> §Paterick TE, Umland MM, Jan MF, et al. Left ventricular noncompaction: a 25-year odyssey. _J Am Soc Echocardiogr_ . 2012;25:363. BPetersen SE, Selvanayagam JB, Wiesmann F, et al. Left ventricular non-compaction: insights from cardiovascular magnetic resonance imaging. _J Am Coll Cardiol_ . 2005;46:101. \n\n¶Jacquier A, Thuny F, Jop B, et al. Measurement of trabeculated left ventricular mass using cardiac magnetic resonance imaging in the diagnosis of left ventricular non-compaction. _Eur Heart J._ 2010;31:1098. _C._ Compacted, _LV,_ left ventricular, _LVNC,_ left ventricular noncompaction, _NC,_ noncompacted. Modified from Paterick TE, Umland MM, Jan MF, et al. Left ventricular noncompaction: a 25-year odyssey. _J Am Soc Echocardiogr_ . 2012:25:363. From Hershberger RE. The dilated, restrictive, and infiltrative cardiomyopathies. In: Libby P, Bonow RO, Mann DL, et al., eds. _Braunwald’s Heart Disease_ . 12th ed. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2022 (ETable 52.3, p. 1041e2). \n\n###### **TABLE** \n\n#### **Causes of LVNC** \n\n###### **28.7** \n\nSarcomeric \n\n**Metabolic Disorder** Barth syndrome Mitochondrial disorders \n\n**Other Genetic** _ZASP HCN4_ α-Actinin-2 \n\n_LVNC,_ Left ventricular noncompaction cardiomyopathy. Reprinted with permission _Circulation_ . 2019;140:e9-e68 ©2019 American Heart Association, Inc. \n\n### **Alström Syndrome** \n\n_ALMS1_ related Alström syndrome is an autosomal recessive ciliopathy that is characterized by progressive sensorineural hearing loss, progressive visual impairment from cone-rod dystrophy, obesity, short stature, insulin resistance, nonalcoholic fatty liver disease, chronic kidney disease, and DCM. Pulmonary involvement also occurs. Systemic fibrosis can occur with increasing age. The absence of significant intellectual disability, polydactyly, or hypogonadism in addition to the presence of cardiomyopathy differentiate Alström syndrome from Bardet-Biedl syndrome, another ciliopathy. _ALMS1_ encodes a protein that localizes to the basal body of cilia as well as the centrosome and may play a\n\n<!-- p.641 -->\n\n###### **TABLE** \n\n###### **28.8** \n\n#### **Revised Task Force Criteria for the Diagnosis of Arrhythmogenic Right Ventricular Cardiomyopathy** \n\n##### **Diagnostic Terminology for Revised Criteria** \n\n- Definite diagnosis: 2 major criteria, or 1 major and 2 minor criteria, or 4 minor criteria from different categories \n\n- Borderline: 1 major criterion and 1 minor criterion, or 3 minor criteria from different categories \n\n- Possible: 1 major criterion, or 2 minor criteria from different categories \n\n##### **I. Global and/or Regional Dysfunction and Structural Alterations** \n\n##### Major Criteria \n\nBy two-dimensional echocardiography: Regional RV akinesia, dyskinesia, or aneurysm and one of the following (end diastole): Parasternal long-axis view of RVOT (PLAX) ≥32 mm (≥19 mm/m<sup>2</sup> ) Parasternal short-axis view of RVOT (PSAX) ≥36 mm (≥21 mm/m<sup>2</sup> )[DF12] \n\n_Or_ \n\nFractional area change <33% By MRI: \n\nRegional RV akinesia, dyskinesia, or dyssynchronous RV contraction and one of the following: Ratio of RVEDV to BSA ≥110 mL/m<sup>2</sup> (male) or ≥100 mL/m<sup>2</sup> (female) or RVEF ≤40% By RV angiography: Regional RV akinesia, dyskinesia, or aneurysm \n\n##### Minor Criteria \n\nBy two-dimensional echocardiography: Regional RV akinesia or dyskinesia and one of the following (end diastole): PLAX RVOT ≥29 to <32 mm (≥16 to <19 mm/m<sup>2</sup> ) PSAX RVOT ≥32 to <36 mm (≥18 mm to <21 mm/m<sup>2</sup> )[DF13] \n\n_Or_ \n\nFractional area change >33 to ≤40% \n\nBy MRI: \n\nRegional RV akinesia, dyskinesia, or dyssynchronous RV contraction and one of the following: \n\nRatio of RVEDV to BSA ≥100 to <110 mL/m<sup>2</sup> (male) or ≥100 to <110 mL/m<sup>2</sup> (female) or RVEF <40 to ≤45% \n\n##### **II. Tissue Characterization of Wall** \n\n##### Major Criteria \n\nResidual myocytes <60% by morphometric analysis (or <50% If estimated), with fibrous replacement of RV free wall myocardium in at least one sample, with or without fatty replacement of tissue seen on endomyocardial biopsy \n\n##### Minor Criteria \n\nResidual myocytes 60–75% by morphometric analysis (or 50–65% if estimated), with fibrous replacement of RV free wall myocardium in at least one sample, with or without fatty replacement of tissue seen on endomyocardial biopsy \n\n##### **III. Repolarization Abnormalities** \n\nMajor Criteria \n\nInverted T waves in right precordial leads (V1, V2, and V2) or beyond in individuals >14 years of age (in absence of complete right bundle branch block QRS ≥120 msec) \n\n##### Minor Criteria \n\nInverted T waves in leads V1 and V2 In Individuals >14 years of age (in absence of complete right bundle branch block) or in V1 V2 or V2 \n\nInverted T waves in leads V1 V2 V2 and V1 in individuals >14 years of age in presence of complete right bundle branch block \n\n##### **IV. Depolarization/Conduction Abnormalities** \n\n##### Major Criteria \n\nEpsilon wave (reproducible low-amplitude signals between end of QRS complex to onset of T wave) in right precordial leads (V1-V2) \n\n##### Minor Criteria \n\nLate potentials by signal-averaged ECG in at least 1 of 3 parameters in absence of QRS duration ≥110 msec on standard ECG Filtered QRS duration ≥114 msec \n\nRoot mean square voltage of terminal 40 msec <20 μV \n\nTerminal activation duration of QRS ≥55 msec measured from nadir of S wave to end of QRS complex, including R in V1 V2 or V2 In absence of complete right bundle branch block \n\n_BSA,_ Body surface area _ECG,_ electrocardiogram; _PLAX,_ parasternal long-axis; _PSAX,_ parasternal short-axis; _RVEDV,_ RV end-diastolic volume; _RVEF,_ RV ejection fraction; _RVOT,_ RV outflow tract. \n\nModified from Marcus Fl, McKenna WJ, Sherrill D, et al. Diagnosis of arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy/dysplasia: proposed modification of the Task Force Criteria. _Eur Heart J_ . 2010;31:806. \n\nFrom Hershberger RE. The dilated, restrictive, and infiltrative cardiomyopathies. In: Libby P, Bonow RO, Mann DL, et al., eds. _Braunwald’s Heart Disease_ . 12th ed. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2022 (eTable 52.2, p. 1039.e2).\n\n<!-- p.642 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 28.9** Causes of Arrhythmogenic Cardiomyopathy\n\n|AVC|\n|---|\n|**Cardiomyopathy Caused by Channelopathy**|\n|LQTS<br>Brugada syndrome<br>Catecholaminergic polymorphic VT<br>SQTS<br>Lenègre disease|\n|Tachycardiaand pacing-induced cardiomyopathy|\n|_AVC,_Arrhythmogenic ventricular cardiomyopathy;_LQTS,_long-QT<br>syndrome;_SQTS,_short-QT syndrome;_VT,_ventricular tachycardia.<br>Reprinted with permission_Circulation_. 2019;140:e9-e68 ©2019<br>American Heart Association, Inc.|\n\n\nrole in centriole structure and function. The exact molecular pathogenesis caused by variants in _ALMS1_ is not known. \n\nCardiomyopathy due to Alström syndrome can present at a wide range of ages from neonatal to adult although the classic presentation is with DCM and heart failure in infancy followed by transient resolution with recurrence in adolescence. Because individuals with Alström syndrome do not have pathognomonic dysmorphic features and other signs and symptoms are age-related in onset, presentation with DCM in infancy is often mistaken for myocarditis or isolated cardiomyopathy unless genetic testing is pursued. Many of the phenotypic features of Alström syndrome overlap with mitochondrial disorders. \n\nA prospective study focusing on the cardiac features in 38 patients with Alström syndrome demonstrated that global longitudinal strain, as diagnosed with cardiac magnetic resonance imaging, was abnormal in 94%, suggesting _subclinical_ cardiac muscle disease even in those without overt cardiomyopathy. In addition, approximately 65% had electrocardiographic abnormalities. \n\n# **Copy Number Variants** \n\n**1p36 deletion** is a common copy number variant associated with syndromic cardiomyopathy, particularly NCM. The size of deletion in 1p36 deletion syndrome is variable. There are several candidate genes within the 1p36 deletion region which may affect the risk for congenital heart defects or cardiomyopathy, especially DCM and NCM. Mapping the critical region for cardiac defects identified _PRDM16_ as a candidate for NCM. _PRDM16_ deletion in animal models is associated with cardiomyopathy and conduction abnormalities. _PRDM16_ conditional knockout mice develop cardiomyopathy with sex-biased penetrance. Similarly, in affected patients, _PRDM16_ deletion is associated with an increased risk of cardiomyopathy. \n\n**6q25.1 deletions** associated with deletion of _TAB2_ have been associated with a syndromic form of cardiomyopathy. The deletion size in _TAB2_ -related disorders is variable, typically ranging from 2 to 10 Mb. The majority of cases are _de_ \n\n_novo_ . _TAB2_ has an important role in outflow tract and cardiac valve formation during embryonic development. Cardiac features include congenital heart defects and characteristic polyvalvular dysplasia including bicuspid aortic valve. Cardiomyopathy is another frequent cardiac finding. Other features include short stature, dysmorphic facial features, and connective tissue abnormalities, especially joint hypermobility, soft skin, hypotonia, hearing loss and myopia. \n\n# **Neuromuscular Diseases** \n\nNeuromuscular diseases are an important etiologic category in pediatric cardiomyopathy (Table 28.10). There are several neuromuscular conditions with specific therapies, some of which overlap with inborn errors of metabolism, and timely diagnosis is important to maximize therapeutic benefit (Fig. 28.1; see Chapter 19). Many neuromuscular diseases will have an elevated serum creatine kinase level; this may be a valuable test in the initial evaluation of a pediatric patient with suspected cardiomyopathy. \n\n# **Muscular Dystrophies** \n\nThe age of onset is variable within and between different muscular dystrophies. If the gene underlying a muscular dystrophy is expressed in both skeletal and cardiac muscle, then there is risk for development of cardiomyopathy, typically DCM, and heart failure (Figs. 28.1 and 28.6). \n\n**Duchenne muscular dystrophy** is an X-linked condition resulting from pathogenic variants in the _DMD_ gene. It affects approximately 1:3,500 live male births and is one of the most common types of muscular dystrophy in childhood. Variants can be _de novo_ or inherited from a carrier mother. Female carriers are at risk for cardiomyopathy and need cardiac surveillance. Dystrophin, the protein encoded by _DMD_ , is extremely large at 3,685 amino acids, and is necessary to maintain the integrity of the muscle cell membrane. In the heart it functions to connect the cytoskeleton to the sarcolemma and extracellular matrix, providing an important scaffold and structural support. Most pathogenic variants are large deletion or duplication events, small insertion-deletion (indel) events resulting in frame deletions or frameshifts, splice site variants, or single nucleotide variants resulting in nonsense variants. Genetic data for 7,149 _DMD_ pathogenic variants are contained in the TREAT-NMD DMD Global database (http://umd.be/ TREAT_DMD/). Typically, dystrophin protein levels are less than 3% in DMD, whereas **Becker muscular dystrophy** is characterized by higher levels of residual protein. For skeletal muscle, the reading-frame rule suggests in-frame variants tend to yield milder phenotypes; cardiac manifestations vary broadly. Onset of skeletal muscle weakness begins in the first few years of life; age 4–5 years is the most common age of diagnosis. Cardiac findings most commonly occur after significant skeletal muscle weakness is evident; it may not be apparent until after the patients are wheelchair bound. Development of myocardial fibrosis, depressed left ventricular ejection fraction, DCM and heart failure occur, ultimately resulting in\n\n<!-- p.643 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 28.10** Gene Variants and Cardiac Manifes tations of Neurom uscular Disorders\n\n|Disorder|Gene Product|Cardiomyopathy|ECG|Arrhythmia|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Duchenne muscular<br>dystrophy (DMD)|Dystrophin|Dilated|Short PR interval, prolonged QT<br>interval, increased QT:PT ratio,<br>right ventricular hypertrophy,<br>deep Q waves II, III, aVF, V5,<br>V6|Increased baseline HR,<br>decreased rate<br>variability, premature<br>ventricular beats|\n|DMD—female carrier|Dystrophin|Dilated|None|Uncommon|\n|Becker muscular dystrophy|Dystrophin|Dilated|Conduction system disease|Similar to DMD|\n|Emery-Dreifuss autosomal<br>dominant or proximal<br>dominant limb-girdle<br>muscular dystrophy IB|Lamin A/C|Dilated|Conduction abnormalities:<br>prolonged PR interval, sinus<br>bradycardia|Atrial fibrillation or<br>flutter and atrial<br>standstill; ventricular<br>dysrhythmias|\n|Limb-girdle muscular<br>dystrophy|α,β,γ,δ<br>sarcoglycans|Dilated|Incomplete right bundle branch<br>block, tall R waves in VIand<br>V2, left anterior hemiblock|Uncommon|\n|Congenital muscular<br>dystrophy|Laminin α2|Dilated|None|None|\n|Limb-girdle muscular<br>dystrophy 21|Fukutin|Dilated|AV node and bundle branch<br>block; age at onset: late<br>teens and early 20s|Atrial arrhythmias and/<br>or ventricular<br>arrhythmias|\n|Emery-Dreifuss X-linked|Emerin|Rare|Conduction abnormalities:<br>prolonged PR interval, sinus<br>bradycardia|Atrial fibrillation or<br>flutter and atrial<br>standstill|\n|Friedreich ataxia|Frataxin|Hypertrophic|T-wave inversion, left axis<br>deviation, repolarization<br>abnormalities|Ventricular arrhythmias|\n|Myotonic dystrophy type 1,<br>infantile|Myotonic<br>dystrophy<br>protein kinase|Hypertrophic|Conduction disease, prolonged<br>PR interval, widening of QRS<br>complex|Atrial fibrillation and<br>flutter, complete<br>heart block|\n|Myotonic dystrophy type 1|Myotonic<br>dystrophy<br>protein kinase|Noncompaction|Conduction disease, prolonged<br>PR interval, widening of QRS<br>complex|Atrial fibrillation and<br>flutter, complete<br>heart block|\n\n\n_AV,_ Atrioventricular; _HR,_ heart rate; _LVNC,_ left ventricular noncompaction. Modified from Hsu DT. Cardiac manifestations of neuromuscular disorders in children. _Pediatr Respir Rev_ . 2010;11:35-38 (Table 1, p 37). \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0644_03.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 28.6** Muscular dystrophies with cardiac involvement. Genes that are expressed in both cardiac and skeletal muscle can lead to muscular dystrophy and cardiomyopathy when abnormal. Three major categories of muscular dystrophies associated with cardiomyopathy are Duchenne/Becker, Emery-Dreifuss, and limb-girdle muscular dystrophies. Not shown are congenital muscular dystrophies which may also be associated with cardiomyopathy. These muscular dystrophies have different patterns of weakness based on the specific skeletal muscles affected in each disorder. Muscles primarily affected in each of the muscular dystrophies are shaded in the diagrams.\n\n<!-- p.644 -->\n\ndeath due to cardiopulmonary disease. Antisense oligonucleotide (ASO) therapy is a therapeutic that induces exon skipping for specific classes of pathogenic variants. Other therapies include vector-mediated gene therapy, stop codon readthrough, and gene-editing strategies. \n\n**Emery-Dreifuss muscular dystrophy** results from pathogenic variants in _EMD_ , an X-linked gene, _FHL1_ , an X-linked gene, or _LMNA_ with autosomal dominant inheritance (Table 28.10). The triad of features that characterizes Emery-Dreifuss muscular dystrophy includes early onset joint contractures, progressive muscle weakness, and HCM with cardiac conduction disease. Cardiac conduction disease includes atrial fibrillation, supraventricular and ventricular arrhythmias, and bundle branch block. While the skeletal muscle features and contractures typically develop in the second decade, cardiac features are more common a decade later. Pathogenic variants in _LMNA_ can also cause isolated DCM and autosomal dominant limb-girdle muscular dystrophy. \n\n**Limb-girdle muscular dystrophies** are a group of autosomal recessive and autosomal dominant conditions that together are the fourth most common type of muscular dystrophy (Table 28.10). There are more than 50 genes known to cause limb-girdle muscular dystrophies. \n\n# **Friedreich Ataxia** \n\nFriedreich ataxia is a neurodegenerative disorder typically caused by biallelic expansion of _FXN_ , the gene encoding frataxin; it is an autosomal recessive trinucleotide repeat disorder (Table 28.10). Severity of symptoms correlates with the number of repeats as does the age of earliest symptoms. The average age of onset is 10 to 15 years. Symptoms include ataxia, muscle weakness, impaired coordination, impaired hearing and/or vision, diabetes, and HCM. Gait ataxia is the first symptom in most patients; HCM develops in approximately 65%. In later stages of disease, systolic function may decrease and DCM may develop. Conduction system disease is also a feature of later stage disease. \n\nFriedreich ataxia may also be considered a mitochondrial disorder because it results from abnormal iron handling in the mitochondria with free radical damage and defective mitochondrial respiration. Antioxidant therapy with coenzyme Q10 has been used. Treatments to stabilize or replace frataxin, increase frataxin gene expression, gene editing, gene replacement, as well as treatments designed to enhance mitochondrial function and reduce oxidative stress and treatments to modulate the metabolic pathways controlled by the frataxin protein have all been attempted and are in various stages of development. The first approved therapy specific for Friedreich ataxia, omaveloxone was developed to increase the number and efficiency of mitochondria. \n\n# **Fatty Acid Oxidation Disorders** \n\nAbnormal metabolism of long chain fatty acids can be associated with hypoketotic hypoglycemia, recurrent rhabdomyolysis, and cardiomyopathy. Very long chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency, long chain 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency, trifunctional protein deficiency, and carnitine palmitoyltransferase II deficiency have all been associated with cardiomyopathy (Tables 28.2 and 28.3). \n\n# **Organic Acidemias** \n\n## (see Chapter 45) \n\nThere are a number of organic acidemias associated with cardiomyopathy; examples include propionic acidemia, methylmalonic acidemia, malonic acidemia, and multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency. Propionic acidemia and methylmalonic acidemias are comparatively common organic acidemias within the same pathways. Cardiomyopathy is more common in propionic acidemia. _TAZ_ related **Barth syndrome** , sometimes referred to as 3-methylglutaconic acidemia type II, is an X-linked cause of cardiomyopathy due to abnormal remodeling of cardiolipin, a complex lipid only found in the inner mitochondrial membrane. Abnormal cardiolipin remodeling ultimately results in abnormal mitochondrial structure and function. The observed elevations of 3-methylglutaconic acid are secondary rather than reflecting the primary metabolic defect; Barth syndrome is considered to be a mitochondrial disorder. Extracardiac features seen in patients with Barth syndrome include intermittent neutropenia, skeletal myopathy, and developmental delays. \n\n# **Congenital Disorders of Glycosylation** \n\n## (see Chapter 47) \n\nCongenital disorders of glycosylation are a diverse group of inborn errors of metabolism that result from hypoglycosylation of proteins and lipids. Because this is a critical process for multiple tissues, congenital disorders of glycosylation typically affect multiple organ systems. When the heart is involved, cardiomyopathy is the most common cardiac manifestation. The primary mode of inheritance of congenital disorders of glycosylation is autosomal recessive; DCM is the most common phenotype. In one case series of 11 patients with pathogenic variants in _DOLK_ , encoding dolichol kinase, the patients appeared to have nonsyndromic DCM rather than a multisystem presentation, highlighting that a high level of suspicion is necessary. The biochemical defect was a combined deficiency of protein N-glycosylation and alpha-dystroglycan O-mannosylation. \n\n# **Mitochondrial Disease** \n\n## (see Chapter 48) \n\n# **Metabolic Cardiomyopathies** \n\nThere are a number of inborn errors of metabolism that are associated with cardiomyopathy. \n\nMitochondrial diseases are genetically complex due to pathogenic variants in both nuclear DNA (Table 28.11) and mitochondrial DNA (Table 28.12). Disorders of mitochondrial DNA are further complicated by _heteroplasmy_ , or the\n\n<!-- p.645 -->\n\n## **TABLE Mitochondrial Diseases From Nuclear DNA Associated With Cardiomyopathy and Arrhythmias or 28.11 Conduction Defects*** \n\n|Condition|Genetic Defect|Heart Findings|Other Clinical Features|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|**Isolated Complex Deficie**|**incies**|||\n|Complex I deficiency|Multiple complex I<br>subunit genes,<br>_ACAD9, FOXRED1_|HCM, DCM, LVNC,<br>WPW|Leigh syndrome, FILA. MELAS,<br>leukoencephalopathy, seizures, hypotonia,<br>pigmentary retinopathy, optic atrophy,<br>hearing loss, liver dysfunction|\n|Complex II deficiency|_SDHA, SDHD_|HCM, DCM, LVNC,<br>AF, heart block|Leukoencephalopathy, cerebellar atrophy,<br>seizures, spasticity, myopathy, liver<br>dysfunction, kidney dysfunction|\n|Complex III deficiency|_BCS1L_|HCM|Developmental delay, psychosis, hearing loss|\n|Complex IV deficiency|_SCO2, SURF1, COA5,_<br>_COX14, COX6B1_|HCM, DCM|Leigh syndrome, encephalopathy, ataxia, liver<br>dysfunction, kidney dysfunction|\n|**Mitochondrial Translation**|**Defects**|||\n|GTP-binding protein-3<br>deficiency|_GTPBP3_|HCM, DCM, heart<br>block, WPW|Leigh syndrome, encephalopathy|\n|Mitochondrial translational<br>activator protein deficiency|_MTOI_|HCM, heart block|Encephalopathy, hypotonia|\n|Alanyl-tRNA synthetase<br>deficiency|_AARS2_|HCM|Leukoencephalopathy, myopathy|\n|Tyrosyl-tRNA synthetase<br>deficiency|_YARS2_|HCM|MLASA syndrome|\n|tRNA methyltransferase-5<br>deficiency|_TRMT5_|HCM|Developmental delay, hypotonia, peripheral<br>neuropathy, renal tubulopathy|\n|RNA processing defect|_ELAC2_|HCM, PSVE|Microcephaly, growth deficiency, hearing loss|\n|Mitochondrial ribosomal<br>subunit deficiencies|_MRPS22, MRPL3,_<br>_MRPL44_|HCM, WPW|Leukoencephalopathy, seizures, liver<br>dysfunction, renal tubulopathy|\n|**mtDNA Depletion Syndro**|**mes**|||\n|MNGIE|_TYMP_|Mild or asymptomatic<br>HCM|Leukoencephalopathy, severe gastrointestinal<br>dysmotility, ophthalmoplegia, hearing loss,<br>peripheral neuropathy|\n|F-box protein deficiency|_FBXL4_|Cardiomyopathy,<br>unspecified|Encephalopathy, brain atrophy|\n|Coenzyme Q10biosynthesis<br>defects|_COQ2, COQ4, COQ9_|HCM|Leigh syndrome, encephalomyopathy, retinitis<br>pigmentosa, hearing loss, liver dysfunction,<br>renal tubulopathy|\n|**3-Methylglutaconic Acidu**|**rias**|||\n|Barth syndrome|_TAZ_|HCM, DCM, LVNC,<br>EFE, VT, LQTS|Myopathy, short stature, neutropenia|\n|Dilated cardiomyopathy and<br>ataxia syndrome|_DNAJC19_|DCM, LVNC|Ataxia, optic ataxia, short stature, testicular<br>abnormalities, liver disease|\n|Complex V deficiency|_TMEM70_|HCM|Cataracts, leukodystrophy, ataxia, myopathy,<br>short stature|\n|Sengers syndrome|_AGK_|HCM|Cataracts, myopathy, exercise intolerance,<br>short stature|\n\n\n_*_ Examples of conditions that are associated with heart disease and feature abnormal nuclear DNA are shown, along with the causative molecular defects and clinical findings. The genetic defects noted above are provided as major contributors to the various mitochondrial conditions but are not a comprehensive compilation. \n\n_AF,_ Atrial fibrillation; _DCM,_ dilated cardiomyopathy; _EFE,_ endocardial fibroelastosis; _FILA,_ fatal infantile lactic acidosis; _GTP,_ guanosine triphosphate; _HCM,_ hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; _LQTS,_ long QT syndrome; _MELAS,_ mitochondrial encephalomyopathy, lactic acidosis, and stroke-like episodes; _MLASA,_ myopathy, lactic acidosis, sideroblastic anemia; _MNGIE,_ mitochondrial neurogastrointestinal encephalopathy; _PSVE,_ paroxysmal supraventricular extrasystoles; _LVNC,_ left ventricular noncompaction; _VT,_ ventricular tachycardia; _WPW,_ Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome. \n\nFrom Enns GM. Pediatric mitochondrial diseases and the heart. _Curr Opin Pediatr_ . 2017;29:541-551 (Table 2, p 543).\n\n<!-- p.646 -->\n\n|**TABLE**|<br>**Mitochondrial DNA Abnormalities Associated With Cardiomyopathy and Arrhythmias or Conduction**|\n|---|---|\n|**28.12**|**Defects***|\n\n\n|Condition|Genetic Defect|Heart Findings|Other Clinical Features|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Kearns-Sayre<br>syndrome|mtDNA deletion|HCM, DCM, heart<br>block, PMVT|Progressive external ophthalmoplegia, pigmentary<br>retinopathy, cerebellar ataxia, hearing loss, increased CSF<br>protein, diabetes mellitus, renal tubulopathy|\n|MELAS|tRNA<sup>Leu</sup>point variant|HCM, DCM, LVNC,<br>RCM, heart block,<br>WPW|Encephalopathy, seizures, stroke-like episodes, headaches,<br>hearing loss, myopathy|\n|MERRF|tRNA<sup>Lys</sup>point variant|HCM, DCM, WPW|Myoclonus, seizures, ataxia, optic atrophy, hearing loss,<br>short stature|\n|Complex I<br>deficiency|Multiple complex<br>I subunit genes|HCM, DCM|Leigh syndrome, leukoencephalopathy, seizures, optic<br>atrophy|\n|Complex III<br>deficiency|_MT-CYB_|HCM, DCM,|Exercise intolerance, myopathy, seizures, optic atrophy, short<br>stature|\n|Complex IV<br>deficiency|_MT-CO1, MT-CO2,_<br>_MT-CO3_|HCM, DCM,|Encephalopathy, seizures, pigmentary retinopathy, hearing<br>loss, myopathy, liver dysfunction|\n|Complex V<br>deficiency|_MT-ATP6, MT-ATP8_|HCM|Ataxia, peripheral neuropathy|\n\n\n_*_ Relatively common conditions that are associated with heart disease and feature abnormal mitochondrial DNA are shown, along with the most common molecular defects and clinical findings. Although the most common molecular defects are indicated in the table, in most cases multiple genetic abnormalities can cause similar clinical presentations. _CSF,_ Cerebrospinal fluid; _DCM,_ dilated cardiomyopathy; _HCM,_ hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; _LVNC,_ left ventricular noncompaction; _MELAS,_ mitochondrial encephalomyopathy, lactic acidosis, and stroke-like episodes; _MERRF,_ myoclonic epilepsy with ragged red fibers; _PMVT,_ polymorphic ventricular tachycardia; _RCM,_ restrictive cardiomyopathy; _WPW,_ Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome. From Enns GM. Pediatric mitochondrial diseases and the heart. _Curr Opin Pediatr_ . 2017;29:541-551 (Table 3, p 546). \n\neffect that mitochondria segregate independently which can cause different numbers of abnormal mitochondria in different tissue types. These differing heteroplasmy levels of the same variant can result in different disease presentations. Cardiovascular involvement in mitochondrial disease is an independent predictor of morbidity and early mortality. HCM, DCM and NCM have all been seen in mitochondrial disease (Fig. 28.7). \n\n# **Storage Diseases** \n\n## (see Chapter 46) \n\nInfantile onset **Pompe disease** is a result of glycogen accumulation within the lysosome presenting with profound hypotonia, cardiomyopathy and cardiorespiratory failure in the first year of life. Pompe disease is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the _GAA_ gene, encoding acid alpha-1,4-glucosidase, also known as acid maltase. \n\nEnzyme replacement therapy with alglucosidase alfa is approved by the FDA for Pompe disease. Additional formulations of enzyme replacement such as avalglucosidase alfa and cipaglucosidase alfa (with the chaperone miglustat) are under investigation to enhance treatment in infantile onset Pompe disease. Gene therapy approaches with adeno-associated viral vectors intravenously or via intradiaphragmatic administration are also under investigation. The use of \n\nhematopoietic stem cell progenitor cell mediated lentiviral gene therapy, which involves autologous hematopoietic stem cell transplantation of cells treated ex-vivo, has been proposed for Pompe disease as well. \n\n_LAMP2_ related **Danon disease** is an X-linked disorder caused by abnormal macroautophagy which leads to accumulation of intracellular vacuoles. Danon disease typically presents with cardiac manifestations with varying levels of cognitive impairment and skeletal muscle weakness, most frequently proximal muscle weakness. Disease penetrance is considered complete, but females with pathogenic variants in _LAMP2_ typically have cardiac manifestations at later ages and often have DCM rather than HCM which is typically seen in males with Danon disease. \n\n**Mucopolysaccharidoses** refer to a class of lysosomal storage disease that is associated with accumulation of glycosaminoglycans. HCM and DCM have been described in mucopolysaccharidoses. Many forms of mucopolysaccharidoses have treatment options with enzyme replacement therapy and/or hematopoietic stem cell transplantation. \n\n# **Risk Stratification and Outcomes** \n\nIn DCM and HCM, there is significant interest in the potential to stratify risk based on genetic diagnosis (Table 28.13). De novo variants have been associated with increased risk of poor outcome. For HCM, major adverse\n\n<!-- p.647 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0648_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 28.7** Examples of mitochondrial defects in patients with cardiomyopathy. (A) Scanning electron micrograph (SEM) from a cardiac biopsy obtained from a 7-year-old male with apparently isolated HCM subsequently shown to have a significantly elevated lactate and abnormal plasma amino acids. SEM shows sheets of intermyofibrillar mitochondria and a paucity of cardiac myofibrils. Mitochondrial genome sequencing revealed m.3303C>T tRNA<sup>Leu</sup> pathogenic variant that was 97% heteroplasmic. (B and C) Skeletal muscle biopsy from a 2-year-old patient with mitochondrial DNA depletion. Presentation was at 6 months of age with cardiogenic shock and arrhythmia, metabolic acidosis, and severe growth restriction. Histology (B) shows abnormalities including central nuclei _(arrows)_ and degeneration _(asterisk)_ . SEM (C) shows pathological mitochondrial throughout including increased neutral lipid and glycogen (not shown in panel), Z-band streaming, increased mitochondrial size, and bizarre shaped mitochondria _(arrows)_ . \n\ncardiac events (MACE) were higher with _MYH7_ pathogenic variants and patients had earlier disease onset. Investigation of lethal arrhythmic events in patients with HCM have shown that adult risk predictors do not perform well in the pediatric population. When comparing children based on underlying cause of HCM, the poorest outcomes were found in patients with inborn errors of metabolism or patients presenting as infants. The rate of death or transplantation was 21% at 2 years in HCM patients diagnosed in infancy. For children with a malformation syndrome, the rate was 23%. Individuals with HCM combined with DCM or RCM had an increased risk of death or heart transplantation. The presence of multiple risk factors increased the likelihood of an adverse event. In contrast, patients with primary or idiopathic HCM after age 1 year had a 3% rate of death or transplant. \n\nIn DCM, risk stratification is based on phenotypic features. A study by the Pediatric Cardiomyopathy Registry showed that the overall 5-year incidence rate of sudden cardiac death is 3%. Patients who met three criteria of age less \n\nthan 14.3 years, left ventricular dilation, and posterior wall thinning were at high risk of sudden cardiac death and consideration for implantable cardioverter-defibrillator placement was recommended. Sex, ethnicity, cause of DCM, and family history were not associated with SCD. When comparing individuals with familial autosomal dominant DCM with idiopathic DCM, the risk of death was lower for those with familial DCM. Older age, congestive heart failure, and greater left ventricular dilation at diagnosis were independently associated with increased risk of death or transplantation. \n\n# **Diagnostic Evaluation and Therapy** \n\n_Consensus guidelines recommend genetic testing for all pediatric patients with cardiomyopathy._ Cardiac surveillance and cascade genetic testing, if indicated, is recommended for first degree relatives of affected individuals. At-risk first-degree relatives who test negative for a known familial pathogenic variant do not require ongoing cardiac surveillance. An approach to genetic screening is noted in Figure 28.8.\n\n<!-- p.648 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 28.13** Specia i Includ l Cases for Genotype-Specific Considerations in ing Genes Associated With Unique Cardiac and N i Clinical Man oncardiac F i agement of DCM, ACM, and HCM, eatures\n\n|DILATED,N|ONDILATED,AND ARRHYTHMOGENIC PHENOTYPES|H|YPERTROPHIC PHENOTYPES|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Gene|Genotype-Specific Features|Gene|Genotype-Specific Features|\n|_TTN_|• Increased risk for peripartum cardiomyopathy<br>• Enriched in populations with myocarditis and<br>alcohol-induced cardiomyopathy<br>• Reproductive partners can be offered genetic<br>testing for reproductive planning given severe risks<br>associated with homozygous inheritance in<br>offspring|_PRKAG2_|• Ventricular pre-excitation and<br>supraventricular arrhythmias<br>• Frequently causes chronotropic<br>incompetence and advanced<br>heart block necessitating<br>pacemaker implantation|\n|_LMNA_*|• Near 100% penetrance<br>• High burden of progressive malignant<br>bradyarrhythmias and tachyarrhythmias in addition<br>to heart failure<br>• High risk of SCD independent of EF<br>• Lower threshold for ICD<br>• Restrictions on physical activity<br>• Association with neuromuscular disease<br>|_LAMP2_*|• Danon disease<br>• X-linked inheritance; affects<br>males>females<br>• Pre-excitation<br>• Skeletal weakness and<br>intellectual disability<br>• Clinical trial ongoing using gene<br>therapy|\n||• Clinical trial ongoing for disease-modifying therapy<br>• Reproductive partners should have prenatal<br>genetic testing given severe risks associated with<br>homozygous inheritance in offspring|_GLA_*|• Fabry disease<br>• X-linked inheritance; affects<br>males>females<br>• Multisystem manifestations<br>including kidney disease, stroke,<br>and acroparesthesia<br>• Disease-modifying treatment<br>(enzyme replacement and<br>substrate reduction therapies)<br>confer favorable biochemical<br>response and stabilization or<br>reduction of ventricular<br>hypertrophy|\n|_SCN5A_*|• Multiple phenotypes (Brugada, LQTS, DCM)<br>• Variants leading to new gating pore cause<br>arrhythmogenic DCM responsive to sodium-<br>channel blockade|_GAA_*|• Pompe disease<br>• Multisystem involvement,<br>especially progressive skeletal<br>muscle weakness<br>• Enzyme replacement therapy is<br>available and improves cardiac<br>function|\n|_DSP, PKP2,_<br>_DSC2,_<br>_DSG, JUP_|• Arrhythmogenic phenotypes<br>• High risk of SCD independent of EF<br>• Restrictions on physical activity|_TTR_*|• Hereditary transthyretin<br>amyloidosis<br>• Multisystem manifestations<br>including peripheral and<br>autonomic neuropathy<br>• Disease-modifying treatment<br>available|\n|_FLNC_|• High risk of SCD independent of EF<br>• Lower threshold for ICD<br>• Reproductive partners can be offered genetic<br>testing for reproductive planning given severe risks<br>associated with homozygous inheritance in<br>offspring|||\n|_DMD_*<br>_*_Genotype-specifi<br>_ACM,_Arrhythmog<br>i|• Neuromuscular involvement<br>• Clinical trial ongoing using gene therapy for males<br>with DMD<br>ic drug therapy either available or under investigation<br>enic cardiomyopathy;_DCM,_dilated cardiomyopathy;_EF,_ejection fractio<br>|n;_HCM,_hypertro|phic cardiomyopathy;_ICD,_implantable cardioverter-|\n|defibrillator;_LQTS,_<br>From Kontorovich|long QT syndrome;_SCD,_sudden cardiac death.<br>AR. Approaches to genetic screening in cardiomyopathies._JACC Hear_|_t Fail_. 2023;11(2)|:133-142 (Table 1, p. 139).|\n\n<!-- p.649 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0650_01.jpg)\n\n\n> **Fig. 28.8** Clinical approach to cardiomyopathies. Cardiomyopathy phenotypes can be roughly categorized according to ventricular morphology and function. After ruling out systemic and environmental/lifestyle causes of cardiomyopathy, clinicians should consider genetic etiologies. A family history of heart failure, cardiomyopathy, or sudden death in a first-degree relative suggests a possible monogenic familial disease. Unique additional cardiac (i.e., arrhythmia, conduction defect, or congenital lesion) and extracardiac (i.e., skeletal myopathy) features may guide the genetic evaluation; a genetic testing strategy may start with a focus on the gene(s) specifically linked to a single highly suspected endotype (i.e., _TTR_ gene when hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis is high on the differential, perhaps due to the presence of an abnormal nuclear scintigraphy scan), or may involve use of a broader multi gene panel. Clinicians should ensure that the selected test includes the gene(s) and methodologies (i.e., need for repeat expansion testing for _DMPK_ in patients with suspected myotonic dystrophy type 1) necessary for diagnoses. The _dagger_ indicates the requirement of repeat expansion analysis. _AL,_ Amyloid light chain; _ASD,_ atrial septal defect; _CCD,_ cardiac conduction disease; _CPK,_ creatine phosphokinase; _GI,_ gastrointestinal; _LVH,_ left ventricular hypertrophy; _PS,_ pulmonic stenosis; _SCD,_ sudden cardiac death; _VA,_ ventricular arrhythmia; _WPW,_ Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome; _WT,_ wild-type. (Modified from Kontorovich AR. Approaches to genetic screening in cardiomyopathies. _JACC Heart Fail_ . 2023;11[2]:133-142 [Fig. 2, p. 138].)\n\n<!-- p.650 -->\n\n### **TABLE** Metabolic Testing for Pediatric Cardiomyopathies **28.14** \n\n|Metabolic Test|How It May Be Informative in Cardiomyopathies|\n|---|---|\n|Acylcarnitine profile|This test gives insight for fatty acid oxidation disorders and indirect information on many<br>organic acidemias. Acylcarnitine profiles can be falsely reassuring in the setting of<br>carnitine deficiency.|\n|Carnitine levels, free and total|This test allows you to interpret the acylcarnitine profile with confidence and detects<br>carnitine deficiency. There are primary carnitine uptake disorders as well as secondary<br>carnitine deficiencies that could have etiologies such as an organic acidemia or dietary<br>restrictions.|\n|Plasma amino acids|Amino acid levels can provide insight into issues such as if lactic acidosis is truly present or<br>a sampling artifact issue (alanine) and can help give context to the other metabolic<br>investigation results.|\n|Urine organic acids|Can detect metabolic patterns suggestive of mitochondrial dysfunction and diagnose<br>organic acidemias.|\n|Lactate|Nonspecific, can be elevated in mitochondrial diseases, fatty acid oxidation disorders,<br>some storage disorders, as well as secondary (e.g., hypoxia) and artefactual (e.g.,<br>squeezing) elevation. Readily available at most institutions with rapid results.|\n|Pyruvate|In the setting of elevated lactate levels, lactate to pyruvate ratios can be a nonspecific<br>indicator of mitochondrial dysfunction.|\n|Very long chain fatty acids|Can detect peroxisomal disorders not detectable with other metabolic testing.|\n|Lysosomal storage disease<br>enzyme panels|Can detect lysosomal storage diseases and potentially other storage disorders depending<br>on the specifics of the test. Clinicians need to review the list of included disorders to<br>ensure the full differential diagnosis is covered.|\n|Metabolomics of blood<br>and/or urine|Can detect a broader range of metabolic disorders due to the nonspecific nature of testing.|\n\n\nThere are special considerations for genetic and genomic testing in pediatric cardiomyopathies due to the potential need to include intronic, exonic and mitochondrial DNA with both sequencing and copy number assessment. Friedreich ataxia is caused by a biallelic repeat expansion in the intron of _FXN_ in approximately 98% of cases, which would not be detected by many commercially available genetic tests, including exome sequencing. Even for patients with apparently isolated cardiomyopathy, careful attention to details is critical. For example, deep intronic variants in _MYBPC3_ are a known etiology for cardiomyopathy and may not be covered on all cardiomyopathy panels. _The ideal comprehensive molecular assessment for pediatric cardiomyopathy patients is broad testing that can detect sequence variants and copy number variants across the nuclear and mitochondrial DNA such as genome sequencing._ Genome sequencing may not always be accessible and medical geneticist assessment can assist in identifying the most appropriate test for a specific patient. \n\nFunctional metabolic testing should be thoughtfully considered in all patients presenting with cardiomyopathy, and is a part of a complete evaluation (Table 28.14). Metabolic testing can potentially yield results more quickly than molecular testing and can detect treatable inborn errors of metabolism not yet fully detected. Many commercial cardiomyopathy panels do not include genes for inborn errors of metabolism, meaning treatable disorders can be easily missed without metabolic assessments. \n\nSpecific metabolic testing that should be considered includes urine organic acids, a plasma acylcarnitine profile, free and total carnitine levels, plasma amino acids, lactate, pyruvate, and a lysosomal storage disease enzyme panel. Rather than specific biochemical testing there has been increased use of clinical metabolomic profiles in plasma and urine (as well as other sample types) for identification of inborn errors of metabolism. The potential benefits of metabolomic profiles are the ability to detect a much broader range of inborn errors of metabolism in a single test. Clinicians should be aware that many suggested markers of mitochondrial disease, such as lactate and growth differentiation factor 15 (GDF15), are elevated in the setting of heart failure and cannot be interpreted reliably for diagnostic purposes in the context of cardiomyopathy. \n"});
